The re-runnable set is development data. It holds the held-out set's own 64 cases and grades them against the held-out set's own key, whose answers were read on 2026-10-01 so that the system could be changed to fix what they exposed; a figure here is a reading of a set the system has now seen, and it is not a generalisation figure. Its runs go to runs/wild-dev/<model>/, which is not the directory holding the held-out set's single run. Re-running it, --force and the advocate replay are all allowed for it. It is never part of the combined figure and never part of --set all.

Both development sets are development data, and neither is a test set. The corpus figure is the figure the submission reports; the combined figure is a sum over two sets that were drafted differently and share one template, so it is not a generalisation figure and does not replace the corpus number. The generalisation figures the submission reports belong to the three sealed sets, each run exactly once: the held-out set's, drafted without access to this repository, from the commit tagged freeze-2026-09-30; the second sealed set's, prepared by OpenAI Codex -- whether Codex could read this repository while preparing it is not recorded -- from a commit whose tribunal/, prompts/ and policy/ trees equal those at freeze-2026-10-03; and the third sealed set's, prepared by an independent author and imported on 2026-10-09, from a commit whose tribunal/, prompts/ and policy/ trees equal those at freeze-2026-10-09. Neither figure here is any of them, and no two of the three are ever added to each other.
